    {
      "id": "workspace-graph-node-projection-landing-surface",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Workspace Graph-Node Projection Draft",
      "position": {
        "x": 120,
        "y": -1250
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 320,
      "ports": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "input",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270
        }
      ],
      "handles": [
        {
          "id": "root",
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "direction": "input",
          "dataType": "any",
          "angle": 270,
          "portId": "root"
        }
      ],
      "inputs": [
        {
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "type": "any"
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [],
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Workspace Graph-Node Projection\n\n> **Experimental plan:** this graph records an implementation attempt to inspect in the running workspace. It is not yet the final law of the model.\n\n## Intended experience\n\n1. Say **Create a new graph** or paste a graph address into the workspace.\n2. Twilite places that graph alongside the graphs already open and makes it the active receiving graph.\n3. Each neighboring graph projects its declared, eligible views and ports as handles on its border.\n4. Drag a handle across the workspace and drop it inside the new graph.\n5. Twilite validates the exposure, previews the result, and creates a node owned by the receiving graph.\n6. Save the receiving graph. The source graph remains unchanged.\n\n## Projection hypothesis\n\nAt workspace scale, a **graph document behaves as a macro-node**. Its frame is the node boundary. Its selected declared view supplies its presentation. Its exposed ports become workspace border ports, and handles make those ports draggable. Bridges describe incoming adjacency; portals describe outgoing adjacency. These are projections of the existing graph model, not a parallel semantic model.\n\nInside a graph, ordinary graph semantics continue to apply. The graph owns its nodes and edges. At workspace scale, layout, focus, frame size, border-handle placement, and minimap arrangement belong to workspace state.\n\n## Drop transaction\n\nThe drag payload identifies the source graph, workspace instance, exposed node and port, selected surface or view, semantic type, permitted action, and provenance. A valid drop into another graph creates a new graph-local node and position. It may also create a portal-backed carrier or a graph-local edge when the exposure contract explicitly requires that result.\n\nThe drop does not mutate the source graph, reuse the source graph-local node ID, or invent an edge that crosses graph documents. Source identity survives as provenance; the receiving graph owns the created node.\n\n## Rendering rule\n\nBorder handles remain stable while the graph changes semantic zoom internally. Incoming bridge-related apertures occupy the top or left. Outgoing or creatable exposures occupy the right or bottom. Unexposed plumbing remains hidden in browse mode. The workspace minimap shows graph frames and their adjacency, while a graph-level minimap can still describe a large focused graph.\n\n## Questions this attempt must answer\n\n- Does a given exposure create a semantic node, a portal-backed carrier, a selected view projection, or present a chooser?\n- Which grants distinguish view consumption, local projection, semantic instantiation, and relationship creation?\n- Which provenance fields disambiguate repeated drops and multiple workspace instances of one source graph?\n\nThe first implementation should make those decisions explicit in one projection resolver and one target-graph transaction, then let us inspect the interaction before promoting the experiment into a durable contract."
        },
        "interfaceContract": {
          "version": 1,
          "receivesViewContent": true
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "workspace-graph-node-projection-declaration"
        }
      }
    },
